The code statements that lead to the bug are:
list_splice_init(&pernet->endp_list, &free_list);
Reason:
This operation modifies the per‑net list head without RCU safety. Since other code traverses the list under rcu_read_lock, the non‑atomic splice can cause a reader to see an inconsistent state, leading to crashes.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
free_list = pernet->endp_list;
INIT_LIST_HEAD_RCU(&pernet->endp_list);
synchronize_rcu();
free_list.prev->next = &free_list;
free_list.next->prev = &free_list;
Reason:
The old list head is copied and the per‑net list is reinitialized under RCU semantics. After the lock is released, synchronize_rcu() guarantees all RCU readers have finished before the old list is processed. Finally, the pointers of the first and last elements are updated to make the copied head a valid standalone list for safe cleanup.

